## Proof

Let $\bar{x}$ denote the unique fixed point of $f$, and define the error terms
$$e_k := \|y^{(k)} - \bar{x}\|, \qquad k \ge 0.$$
Also define the running average
$$s_k := \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} y^{(i)}, \qquad k \ge 1,$$
so that $y^{(k)} = f(s_k)$.

**Key recurrence.** Since $f(\bar{x}) = \bar{x}$ and $f$ is a contraction with constant $c$,
$$e_k = \|f(s_k) - f(\bar{x})\| \le c\,\|s_k - \bar{x}\| \le c\cdot\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\|y^{(i)}-\bar{x}\| = \frac{c}{k}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} e_i. \tag{1}$$

**Bounding the partial sums.** Let $S_k := \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} e_i$ (so $S_1 = e_0 = \|x_0 - \bar{x}\|$). From (1),
$$S_{k+1} = S_k + e_k \le S_k + \frac{c}{k}S_k = S_k\!\left(1 + \frac{c}{k}\right), \qquad k \ge 1.$$

Iterating,
$$S_k \le e_0\prod_{j=1}^{k-1}\!\left(1+\frac{c}{j}\right), \qquad k \ge 1. \tag{2}$$

**Growth rate of the product.** Using $\log(1+t) \le t$,
$$\log\prod_{j=1}^{k-1}\!\left(1+\frac{c}{j}\right) = \sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\log\!\left(1+\frac{c}{j}\right) \le c\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\frac{1}{j} \le c\,(1+\log k).$$

Hence from (2),
$$S_k \le e_0\, e^{c}\, k^{c}. \tag{3}$$

**Conclusion.** Applying (1) once more with (3):
$$e_k \le \frac{c}{k}\,S_k \le \frac{c\,e_0\,e^{c}}{k}\,k^{c} = c\,e_0\,e^{c}\,k^{c-1}.$$

Since $c \in (0,1)$, the exponent $c-1 < 0$, so
$$\lim_{k\to\infty} e_k = \lim_{k\to\infty} c\,e_0\,e^{c}\,k^{c-1} = 0.$$

Therefore
$$\lim_{k\to\infty} y^{(k)} = \bar{x},$$
i.e., **the sequence $y^{(k)}$ converges to the fixed point $\bar{x}$**. $\blacksquare$

